<%#
  Read-only monitor config, with its source named (v1-scope §3.3). There is no
  edit form any more — `bin/rails stablemate:sync` is the only writer of monitor
  config — so this panel must answer the question the vanished Edit button
  raises: where does this setting live, and how do I change it?

  The copy branches on `source` because §8's backfill leaves a permanent
  `manual` population that no repo defines, for which every sentence about the
  gem is false. Locals: monitor.
%>
<div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 shadow-sm" data-testid="config-panel">
  <h2 class="text-[13px] font-semibold text-secondary mb-4">Settings</h2>
  <dl class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-y-3 text-[13px]">
    <dt class="text-muted">Status</dt><dd><%= render "shared/status_badge", status: monitor.status %></dd>
    <dt class="text-muted">Expected interval</dt><dd class="font-mono"><%= humanize_seconds(monitor.expected_interval_seconds) %></dd>
    <dt class="text-muted">Grace period</dt><dd class="font-mono"><%= humanize_seconds(monitor.grace_period_seconds) %></dd>
    <dt class="text-muted">Last ping</dt>
    <dd class="font-mono"><%= mono_timestamp(monitor.last_ping_at) %></dd>
    <%= render "monitors/next_check", monitor: monitor, variant: :dl %>
  </dl>

  <div class="mt-4 border-t border-hairline pt-3 text-[12px] text-muted leading-relaxed" data-testid="config-source">
    <% if monitor.retired? %>
      <%# A retired monitor's task is NOT in the repo — that absence is why it
          was pruned. Telling its owner to "edit it where it lives and deploy"
          points at a file that no longer contains the task, and contradicts the
          dashboard, which correctly says to restore it. %>
      <p>
        <span class="font-medium text-secondary">No longer declared in your app's config.</span>
        Its settings are the ones it had when
        <code class="font-mono text-secondary">stablemate:sync</code> last saw it.
        Restore the task and the next sync brings the monitor back with its
        history intact.
      </p>
    <% elsif monitor.from_gem? %>
      <p>
        <span class="font-medium text-secondary">Defined in your repo.</span>
        <%# §11: the schedule string may be SHOWN when present, but nothing may be
            PROMISED from it — detection is interval-based in V1, so this panel
            never claims a cron-derived next run. When the column is NULL (a
            `c.monitors` interval declaration) we fall back to the mechanism
            line, which is true of every synced row. %>
        <% if monitor.schedule.present? %>
          Checked every <%= humanize_seconds(monitor.expected_interval_seconds) %>,
          derived from <code class="font-mono text-secondary"><%= monitor.schedule %></code>
          <% if monitor.last_synced_app.present? %>in <%= monitor.last_synced_app %><% end %>.
        <% else %>
          Registered by <code class="font-mono text-secondary">stablemate:sync</code><%
            %><% if monitor.last_synced_app.present? %> from <%= monitor.last_synced_app %><% end %>.
        <% end %>
        Edit it where it lives and deploy — the next sync applies the change.
      </p>
      <% if monitor.schedule.present? %>
        <p class="mt-1">
          Stablemate watches the gap between runs, not the calendar, so the
          interval above is what it actually enforces.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    <% elsif monitor.manual? %>
      <%# §11: the backfilled population has no config writer BY DEFAULT — no
          form, no derived sync entry (the gem never derives a `manual-<id>` key
          from a job class), and no transfer. Say so here rather than leave the
          owner of a pre-CLI monitor to work it out from a missing button.

          Deliberately NOT "its settings can no longer be changed", which is
          false: nothing filters the sync's upsert by `source`, so declaring this
          row's key in `c.monitors` adopts it and writes its settings in place.
          Claiming otherwise would push the owner into the destructive remedy —
          delete-and-redeclare throws away every ping event, incident and uptime
          day the monitor has — for a change that adoption makes without losing
          any of it. Both routes are offered, cheapest first. %>
      <p>
        <span class="font-medium text-secondary">Created before Stablemate moved to config-as-code</span>,
        so nothing in a repo declares it and
        <code class="font-mono text-secondary">stablemate:sync</code> leaves it alone.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-1">
        To bring it under your repo, declare
        <code class="font-mono text-secondary"><%= monitor.registration_key %></code>
        in <code class="font-mono text-secondary">c.monitors</code> — the next sync
        adopts this monitor and keeps its history. To move it onto a name you
        choose instead, delete it and declare the work afresh; that starts its
        history over.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
